{"id": "da6cddb5-75df-45ca-8963-674d5f8840df", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:16:18.363122", "source": "user", "parent_id": "cb969bbb-dfeb-4aad-9ad4-c1f18c2e6d22", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Ок, осмотр принят: /workspace пуст (кроме task.json), нет torch/numpy/pandas, DNS мёртв → pip невозможен, GPU нет, датасетов нет. Это закрытый вопрос, исполнение откладываем — задача design-only, и это согласовано.\\n\\nПодход в целом годный, механизмы CycleNet описаны правильно, baseline-набор и особенно seasonal naive + sensitivity по L ты попал в точку — именно это отличает проверку гипотезы от «построили ещё один MLP». Approve, работаем дальше. Но перед тем как писать финальный дизайн, зафиксируй четыре вещи, они прямо влияют на структуру документа:\\n\\n1) Бюджет неизвестен — не гадай за меня. Сделай дизайн тиernами: Tier A (минимально достаточный: какие 2 датасета, какие 2-3 модели, какие 3 абляции, что именно отвечает на вопрос) и Tier B (полный: 4 датасета, все horizons, трансформер-линия, experiment по пропускам). К каждому тиеру — грубая оценка GPU-часов по формулам/порядку (param count × epochs × windows), без выдуманного SOTA-времени. Я сама решу, что тянуть.\\n\\n2) Пропуски/нерегулярная дискретизация: это отдельный эксперимент с собственной гипотезой и своей baseline-сеткой, а не строчка в таблице. Реши и аргументируй: он в Tier A или в Tier B. Мой аргумент за Tier B — в валидных бенчмарках пропусков нет, придётся синтетически прореживать, а это отдельный источник confound (какой механизм missing — MCAR по фазе или блоками; block-missing ломает саму фазовую агрегацию, и тогда непонятно, кто деградировал). Пропиши, как ты это разводишь.\\n\\n3) Детали паттерн-банка и gaiding loss ты сам поместил в «сверить с первоисточником». Так и оставь: в документе они должны быть явно маркированы как параметризуемые допущения с именованными hyper-параметрами (размер банка, способ агрегации, вес gaiding-члена, K гармоник), а не как «так в статье». Никаких ссылок на цифры/результаты из статьи, которых мы не открывали.\\n\\n4) Гипотезы должны быть фальсифицируемы. Для каждого анализа напиши заранее, какой результат его опровергает. Например: если при L±1 или намеренно неверной L MSE не хуже, чем при верной — эффект не от периодики, и вывод это обязан допустить. То же для вклада periodic-вклада при обнулённом банке. Не подгоняй пороги постфактум; если порог сам по себе假设 — так и подпиши.\\n\\nПо технике, два конкретных запроса к плану анализа:\\n- MSE/MAE на RevIN-нормализованных данных — это соглашение, но тогда baseline-цифры из внешних таблиц напрямую несопоставимы. Пропиши либо denormalised-метрики как основные, либо явную оговорку про несравнимость; выбери одно и обоснуй.\\n- 2-3 сида — это очень мало для доверительных интервалов. Либо честно назови это screening run без заявлений о значимости, либо добавь дешёвый тест по окнам (paired-сравнение на одинаковых test-окнах, paired bootstrap), которое реально различит CycleNet vs DLinear. Последнее предпочтительнее, оно почти бесплатное.\\n\\nETTm период ты сам пометил «уточнить» — не оставляй висяком: в дизайне период задаётся как функция частоты дискретизации, формула + таблица значений по датасетам, и источник — timestamps, не выдуманная колонка.\\n\\nИтоговый артефакт: один самодостаточный спецификационный документ — постановка и гипотезы, архитектура CycleNet с явными hyper-параметрами и маркированными допущениями, данные/сплиты/протокол, полная сетка сравнений и абляций с обоснованием каждой строки, план анализа с критериальными исходами, оценка вычислительной стоимости по тиерам, и раздел «что нельзя проверить в текущем sandbox и почему». Код писать не нужно, исполнение не требуется. Если хочешь, можешь приложить расчётный feasibility-check (память/время по формулам) — это допустимо и у нас реально считается, но без имитации обучения.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "c90eb58c-b26f-4be3-8e1e-5e81451ad33c", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:20:47.224361", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "da6cddb5-75df-45ca-8963-674d5f8840df", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Принято. Сначала сделаю расчётную часть (окна, параметры, FLOP-порядки по формулам — арифметика stdlib, без имитации обучения), чтобы оценки по тирам были не «на глаз»."}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher approved and requested the complete design (stage=result). There are several requirements:\n\n1. Tiering: Tier A (minimum: 2 datasets, 2–3 models, 3 ablations) and Tier B (full: 4 datasets, all horizons, transformer line, missing-data experiment). Rough GPU-hour estimates per tier via formulas / order-of-magnitude, without fabricating SOTA times.\n2. Missing-data experiment: decide whether Tier A or Tier B, with argumentation. The researcher argues Tier B, since synthetic subsampling introduces confounding (MCAR vs block-missing; block-missing breaks phase aggregation). I should agree with Tier B and specify how to disentangle the MCAR vs block mechanism.\n3. Pattern bank / guidance-loss details: mark as explicit tunable assumptions with named hyperparameters (bank size, aggregation scheme, guidance-loss weight, K harmonics). Do not cite paper numbers.\n4. Falsifiable hypotheses: for each analysis, describe in advance the result that would refute it. Thresholds marked as assumptions.\n\nTechnical requests:\n- RevIN normalized metrics vs de-normalized: choose one and justify. Proposal: de-normalized (original scale) metrics as primary for cross-method comparability with external tables, plus normalized as secondary; actually the researcher says \"choose one and justify.\" Hmm. Standard practice: ETT benchmarks report per-channel normalized MSE/MAE (actually the standard protocol normalizes the whole dataset with train-set mean/std via sklearn StandardScaler, and RevIN does instance normalization). The comparability issue concerns external tables. De-normalized metrics are dominated by high-variance channels (e.g., OT in ETT). Choice: normalized as primary (robust across channels, standard within our grid since all our methods use the same protocol — all internally comparable), and explicitly note non-comparability with external tables, plus report de-normalized as appendix. Actually the researcher said to choose one as primary and either de-normalize or explicitly disclaim non-comparability. My choice: normalized primary + explicit disclaimer + de-normalized secondary per dataset/channel. Justification: cross-channel comparability within our comparison grid matters more than compatibility with external tables, since our claims are internal comparisons (CycleNet vs baselines run under the same protocol).\n\n- Seeds: 2–3 seeds = screening; add paired per-window comparison: paired bootstrap over test windows for CycleNet vs DLinear (and vs seasonal naive). Nearly free. Paired tests across seeds too.\n\n- ETT periods: formula L = P / Δ, where P = natural-cycle length (daily=24h; weekly=168h) and Δ = sampling interval. Table: ETTh1/ETTh2 Δ=1h → daily L=24, weekly L=168; ETTm1/ETTm2 Δ=10min → daily L=144, weekly L=1008. Weather Δ=10min → L=144 (daily), 1008 weekly. Traffic Δ=1h → L=24 daily? Traffic is usually hourly, weekly period 168. Electricity Δ=1h → weekly L=168. Source: timestamps column, dataset metadata; L chosen by validation.\n\nGPU-hour estimation: compute by formula. Model parameter counts are on the order of: DLinear ~ O(d_lookback × H) per channel, tiny (~tens of K); PatchTST ~3–10M; CycleNet: input MLP (96→H) + pattern bank L×d_model + guidance MLP. Number of windows: e.g. ETTm1 train ~34×24×6=4896 points, windows = N_train − lookback − horizon + 1 ≈ 4700. Batch 32 → ~150 steps/epoch, 10–20 epochs. These models are tiny; a few minutes on A100 for small datasets; Traffic (52k points, 862 channels) is heavier but still under a few hours. We can give order-of-magnitude formulas: time ≈ epochs × (N_windows/B) × t_step; t_step ≈ param_count × B × lookback × constant. Better: present parameter count + FLOP estimates and an assumed A100 throughput range with an explicit assumption flag (e.g. assume effective 1e12 FLOP/s on an A100 bf16 — but that's a publicly known hardware spec so fine as an assumption).\n\nActually, the researcher asked for a \"feasibility-check by computation (memory/time by formula)\" — \"у нас реально считается (things are actually computed here)\". A python3 script for simple computation (pure stdlib, formulas) can be run as a computation-based feasibility check. Let's do that: compute window counts, parameter estimates, FLOP estimates per epoch, then derive GPU-hour estimates at assumed throughput, clearly marking the assumption. This is legitimate — it's arithmetic, not simulated training.\n\nDataset sizes (publicly known metadata — sizes should be marked as approximate/verify-on-download; since we can't download we mark as assumption):\n- ETTh1/ETTh2: 17,420 rows, 7 columns (hourly).\n- ETTm1/ETTm2: 69,680 rows (10 min), 7 columns.\n- Weather: 52,696 rows, 21 columns (10 min).\n- Traffic: 17,544 rows, 862 columns (hourly).\n- Electricity: 26,304 rows, 321 columns (hourly).\n\nThese are well-known standard numbers but can't be verified offline; mark \"subject to verification upon data acquisition.\"\n\nStandard splits:\n- ETTh: train 12 months = 8,760, val = 8,760 (4 months... actually the standard: train 8760, val 3504? Zeng et al.'s standard: ETTh is 7:1:2 → 12,194/1,732... hmm. Common code (Autoformer): ETTh1: train [0, 8640], val [8640-96, 11760], test [11640, 17400+]. 12 months hourly = 8,640 (12×30×24). val 4 months ≈ 2,880 + lookahead, test 4 months ≈ 2,880+. Simplify by describing: 12/4/4 months (Autoformer convention: 8,640 / 3,504 / 3,504 (incl. overlap)); ETTm: 34/4/5 days? Autoformer: ETTm train [0, 34,560]= 34,560 points (24 days? 34,560×10min = 240 days = 8 months). Hmm. ETTm1 standard split: train 34,560 (240 days), val 11,520 (80 days), test 11,520 (80 days), total 69,680 → 69,680/144 ≈ 484 days ≈ 16 months. Train 34,560/144=240 days, val = 11,520+... OK: 240/80/80 days.\n- Weather: 7:1:2 of 52,696 → 36,884 / 5,272 / 10,540.\n- Traffic: 7:1:2 of 17,544 → 12,280 / 1,752 / 3,512.\n\nExpress the 7:1:2 rule and month/day counts, note that exact boundaries should follow the standard repository at implementation time.\n\nNumber of windows with lookback 96, horizon 724: N_train − (96+724) + 1.\n\nParameter-count formulas:\n- CycleNet (simplified per-channel or multivariate): flatten input 96×C → hidden → H×C. For multivariate: input proj (96C→d_model), phase embedding (2K→d), bank L×d_model, output d_model(+?)→H×C. Rough estimate.\n- DLinear: (lookback+1)×(horizon+1) params per channel? DLinear: two linear maps decomposition→horizon: ~2×96×96×C.\n\nFLOP per step ≈ 2 × param_count × batch_tokens; for linear models FLOPs ≈ 2 × params × batch × C? DLinear is per-channel so ×C. OK — give order-of-magnitude formulas and a computed table, flag the assumption: A100 sustained effective throughput 1e12 FLOP/s (≈5% of bf16 peak — conservative), mark as assumption.\n\nLet's compute concrete numbers with a Python script.\n\nTier A:\n- Datasets: ETTh1 (hourly, cheap), ETTm1 (10-min, tests sub-daily phase resolution). 2 datasets, both 7 channels — tiny.\n- Models: seasonal naive, DLinear, CycleNet full + 3 ablations (no guidance loss, no bank (no phase input), no phase Fourier encoding... researcher said 3 ablations). Actually ablations are variants of CycleNet.\n- Horizons: {96, 724}? Researcher: \"какие horizons (which horizons)\" — for Tier A use {96, 336} or {96, 724}. Let's go with 96 and 724 (short/long endpoints).\n- Plus L-sensitivity sweep and zero-bank decomposition — this is the falsification core of Tier A.\n- Paired bootstrap over test windows.\n\nTier A GPU: ETTh1 train 8,640 points → ~8,000 windows. DLinear/CycleNet: <1M params; steps/epoch = 8000/32=250; FLOP/epoch ≈ 2×1e6×32×250×... ~ 2×params×batch×steps = 2×1e6×8000 ≈ 1.6e10 FLOP/epoch. Negligible. 20 epochs → 3e11 FLOP → sub-second on A100; realistically dominated by data loading: a few minutes per run. Tier A total ≈ <2–3 GPU-hours even generously.\n\nTier B:\n- Add Weather (21 ch, 52k), Traffic (862 ch, 17.5k), horizons {96,192,336,724}, transformer line PatchTST (and/or Autoformer), all 5 ablations, 3 seeds, missing-data experiment (Weather subsampled MCAR + block-missing), visualization/decomposition.\n- Traffic multivariate: C=862, input 96×862=82,752 → DLinear 862×96×H ≈ 862×96×724 ≈ 6e7 params; fine. FLOP/epoch = 2×6e7×(train_windows≈11,000) ≈ 1.3e12 → still tiny. PatchTST ~5–10M params but per-channel batching: tokens = windows×C batches... PatchTST on Traffic is a real load: typically a few hours per dataset-horizon. Estimate: 3–8 GPU-hours per run → 4 horizons × 3 seeds = 36–96 GPU-hours. Tier B total order 100–300 GPU-hours; present the formula-derived table with assumption flags.\n\nLet's actually run the arithmetic in python to generate the table (this is our \"расчётный feasibility-check (computational feasibility check)\").\n\nMissing-data experiment decision: agree with Tier B. Argumentation: (i) requires synthetic subsampling — a new data-generating process not present in any benchmark; (ii) missingness mechanism is a real confound: MCAR by phase preserves phase coverage (the bank still sees each phase), block-missing (≥L consecutive) removes entire phase regions and can starve the bank — degrading both CycleNet and seasonal naive, muddying attribution. Design: treat missingness rate ρ ∈ {10,20,50}% × mechanism ∈ {MCAR by timepoint, MCAR by whole cycle (drop every m-th cycle — this specifically removes phase coverage... actually cycle-wise removal removes complete phase sets uniformly... blocks within a cycle}, measure per-phase coverage, report degradation curves per mechanism rather than a single table row; pre-registered directional hypothesis: under equal ρ, CycleNet's relative degradation over seasonal naive increases for MCAR and may decrease for block; refutation criterion: if degradation curves coincide across all mechanisms → claim of robustness is refuted. Tier B because (a) it needs its own baseline grid (seasonal naive, DLinear, PatchTST, CycleNet) × mechanisms × ρ; (b) primary-question answer (does phase modeling help) doesn't need it — it answers a secondary claim; (c) keeps Tier A conclusion set free of confound.\n\nMetric decision: primary = per-channel normalized (dataset-level z-score on train split; identical protocol across all methods; internal comparability), with explicit disclaimer against external tables, plus report de-normalized raw-scale metrics in an appendix table for practitioners. Justification: our hypotheses are all internal comparisons run by ourselves under one protocol; per-channel normalized prevents any single channel from dominating the aggregate (ETT's OT, Traffic's high-flow); de-normalized aggregates across heterogeneous channels are not interpretable. External-table compatibility is not a hypothesis → appendix only. Also note that RevIN (instance) vs global z-score is a choice: use RevIN for all models including linear baselines for fairness, primary metrics computed on the normalized space defined by the per-instance RevIN scale — hmm, per-instance RevIN makes MSE not comparable across instances. Standard practice: report on RevIN-normalized (i.e. each instance is normalized, and the error normalized by that instance's std) — that's what the papers do. OK, keep it: primary = RevIN-normalized (all models share RevIN), external comparability disclaimed; secondary = raw scale, per-channel RMSE/MAE averaged across channels. Actually the researcher asked for either \"de-normalized as primary\" or \"explicit non-comparability disclaimer.\" Choose normalized as primary + disclaimer. OK.\n\nSeeds/statistics: 3 seeds as screening (no CI claims); primary inference = paired comparison over test windows: paired bootstrap (B=10,000 resamplings of window-level squared-error differences, CycleNet vs DLinear, vs seasonal naive), stratified over horizons; paired t-test/Wilcoxon as secondary. Pre-registered: significance = 95% CI of the paired mean difference excludes 0; note caveats about temporal autocorrelation of window errors (block bootstrap with window blocks of, say, 100, to account for serial correlation — good point; a block-paired bootstrap of block length 96 = lookback). Mark block length as an assumption.\n\nPeriod formula: L = natural-cycle seconds / median Δt from timestamps. Table:\n- ETTh1/ETTh2: Δt=1h → daily 24, weekly 168.\n- ETTm1/ETTm2: Δt=10min → daily 144, weekly 1008.\n- Weather: Δt=10min → 144 / 1008.\n- Traffic: Δt=1h → 24 / 168.\n- Electricity: Δt=1h → 24 / 168.\nPrimary L: for hourly-set hourly-sampling horizons 96/724 pick the weekly 168... hmm: for hourly-set horizons 96/724, weekly L=168 is standard as dominant in traffic/electricity, daily 24 for ETT (temperature-driven). Handling of design: candidate L grid = {daily, weekly}, plus sweep L±{1,2}; selection on validation (this itself is a falsification test). Source of period: timestamps column → assert regular grid; if irregular intervals exist, that is a preprocessing fact to be logged.\n\nNamed hyperparameters (tunable-assumption flags):\n- L: cycle length (selected from grid; primary hypothesis test).\n- d_bank: pattern bank feature dim d (bank size L×d).\n- agg ∈ {EMA, uniform running mean}, β_ema ∈ {0.9, 0.99}.\n- λ_guide: guidance-loss weight ∈ {0, 0.1, 1} (0 = ablation).\n- K harmonics ∈ {1, 2, 3} for phase Fourier encoding.\n- Update mode: batch-mean vs online EMA — flagged as \"verify in original paper before implementation.\"\n- RevIN affine on/off, lookback ∈ {96, 512}, batch 32, Adam lr grid, epochs ≤20 with early stopping on val (patience 5).\n\nFalsification criteria (per-analysis pre-registration):\n- H1 (phase modeling helps): CycleNet beats DLinear and seasonal naive in MSE at p<0.05 (block-paired bootstrap) across both datasets and both horizons. Refutation: fails to beat seasonal naive on any dataset-horizon → effect may just be \"knowing L,\" no added value from learned patterns.\n- H2 (effect is period-specific): MSE(L*) < MSE(L±1, L±2, wrong L). Refutation: any perturbed L within the 95% CI (i.e. no worse) → the gain is not attributable to correct periodicity. Note: small datasets are noisy; require that the paired bootstrap also excludes 0.\n- H3 (bank carries the periodic component): zero-bank ablation degrades MSE by more than the guide-bank gap... define Δ_periodic = MSE(zero-bank) − MSE(full); refutation: Δ ≤ 0, or CI covering 0 → periodic component contributes nothing.\n- H4 (guidance loss does the aggregation work): full vs λ_guide=0: refutation: no significant difference → guidance term unnecessary.\n- H5 (Fourier phase code): refutation: learned embedding equal or better → Fourier claim false (this is fine, report either way).\n- H6 (missing-data robustness, Tier B): per mechanism, as above.\n\nThreshold assumption: no effect-size thresholds set post hoc; the only pre-registered criterion is the sign of the paired difference excluding 0. The practical-significance threshold (e.g. \"≥5% MSE improvement\") is flagged as an arbitrary/assumptive convenience, not a hypothesis.\n\nGPU-hour arithmetic: run python computation now, produce concrete table, cite as computational feasibility check (arithmetic, not training).\n\nFormulas:\n- N_windows(dataset, split, τ, H) = N_train − τ − H + 1 (sliding stride 1; stride τ/2 option for training efficiency).\n- Param counts (multivariate, flatten):\n  - DLinear: C×(2×τ×(H)) + bias ≈ 2τHC.\n  - CycleNet-lite: W_in: (τC)×d + W_bank: L×d + W_out: d×(H... plus direct linear path τ×H×C) — approximate 200K–2M.\n  - PatchTST: ~3–6M (shared across channels) + head.\n- FLOP/epoch ≈ 2×P×N_win×E, for dense layers (forward + backward ≈ 3× forward ≈ 6×P×samples). Use 6×P×N_win.\n- Memory: per-sample activations ≈ P_layer × batch... simplify: dominant activation ≈ batch × τ × C × float4 × n_layers; Traffic: 32×96×862×4B ≈ 10.5 MB per layer-activation → negligible even with 6 layers <1GB. Fine on A100 40GB.\n\nRun arithmetic: compute N_train per dataset from row-count assumption, windows for (τ=96, H∈{96,724}) and τ=512, then FLOP at 20 epochs, time at assumed sustained 1e12 FLOP/s with ×5 overhead factor (dataloader inefficiency on CPU-bound tiny models) — honestly, for tiny models wall-clock is overhead-dominated: estimate 3–10 min per run wall-clock on a single A100 (flag as engineering-experience assumption). Tier A run counts: 2 datasets × 2 horizons × 3 seeds × (naive, DLinear, CycleNet) + 3 CycleNet ablations + L-sensitivity (5 values of L × 1 seed?) = run count and time estimates.\n\nRun count:\n- Tier A: datasets {ETTh1, ETTm1}=2; horizons {96,724}=2; models: seasonal naive (no training), DLinear, CycleNet = 2 trainable; ablations 3 (λ=0, no bank, no Fourier); L-sensitivity: 5 additional L values (L±1, L±2, wrong L) CycleNet 1 seed = 5; zero-bank decomposition (inference only, ~0). Trainable runs = (2+3+5... include DLinear? no, L-sensitivity is CycleNet-only) → per dataset-horizon: 2×3 + 3×3 + 5×1 = 6+9+5=20 runs; ×4 (dataset×horizon) = 80 runs ≈ ~5–10 min each (tiny, overhead-dominated) → ~7–13 hours CPU/GPU wall-clock? Hmm, ETTh/ETTm runs are really a few minutes on GPU; 80×7 min ≈ 9.3 GPU-hours. Present as order: 5–15 GPU-hours.\n- Tier B additions: +Weather +Traffic; horizons {96,192,336,724}=4; models + PatchTST + Autoformer; 3 seeds; full ablation grid; missing-data experiment: Weather × mechanisms {MCAR, cycle block} × ρ {10,30,50}% = 6 conditions × 4 trainable models × 1 horizon (724) × 2 seeds = 48 runs, moderate. PatchTST on Traffic heavy: estimate 2–8 GPU-hours per run (flag assumption) × 4 horizons × 3 seeds = 24–96 h. Tier B total order 150–400 GPU-hours; formula + table.\n\nWrite the python script that computes window counts and FLOPs and run it once. Then submit the result.\n\nAlso include \"things that cannot be verified in the current sandbox and why\": installation of packages (DNS dead), downloading data (DNS dead), training (no GPU/packages), verification of dataset files/periods from timestamps (no data), verification of paper equations (no network) — all environment-verified facts.\n\nDocument structure of the answer:\n0. Scope and status\n1. Problem setup and hypotheses H1–H6 with falsification criteria\n2. Architecture CycleNet with named hyperparameters + flagged assumptions (A1–A8)\n3. Data/splits/protocol; period formula + table\n4. Comparison grid Tier A/Tier B and rationale for each row; rationale for missing-data experiment being in Tier B + disentangling mechanisms\n5. Analysis plan: primary metrics decision, paired block bootstrap, L-sensitivity criterion, decomposition, threshold flags\n6. Computational cost by tier (formula + computed table + assumption tags)\n7. Sandbox restrictions\n\nKeep it thorough but organized. Run the arithmetic script first.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cat << 'EOF' > /tmp/calc.py\n# Arithmetic feasibility model (formulas only, no training simulated).\n# Dataset metadata = public approximations, MUST be verified on download (flagged assumption).\nDS = {  # name: (total_rows, n_channels, dt_minutes)\n 'ETTh1': (17420, 7, 60), 'ETTh2': (17420, 7, 60),\n 'ETTm1': (69680, 7, 10), 'ETTm2': (69680, 7, 10),\n 'Weather': (52696, 21, 10), 'Traffic': (17544, 862, 60), 'Electricity': (26304, 321, 60),\n}\ndef split712(n): return int(0.7*n), int(0.1*n), int(0.2*n)\ndef windows(n_train, tau, H): return max(n_train - tau - H + 1, 0)\n\ndef params_DLinear(tau,H,C): return 2*tau*H*C              # per-channel two linear maps (order)\ndef params_CycleNet(tau,H,C,d,L,K=2):\n    return tau*C*d + L*d + d*H*C + tau*H*C + 2*K*d          # in-proj + bank + out + linear path\ndef params_PatchTST(tau,H,C): return 5e6 + (ta